\section{Standard errors, degrees of freedom and intervals}{

\strong{\code{estimatr} and \code{fixest}.} These fits carry the variance they were
computed with, estimatr's \verb{se_type =} and whatever fixest's own \code{vcov}
interface produced, and spicy never overwrites it. A \code{vcov} request
other than \code{"classical"} is refused; set the estimator through the
fitting package instead: \verb{se_type =} (with \verb{clusters =}) for estimatr,
\verb{vcov =} at estimation or in \code{summary()} for fixest. The note carries
fixest's own label of the estimator (clustered, Newey-West, Conley,
...), fixest's "IID" written as "Classical". With IID errors, fixest's
default small-sample correction (\code{\link[fixest:ssc]{fixest::ssc()}}) multiplies the
standard errors of a \code{feglm()} fit by sqrt((n - 1) / (n - K)) over
those of its \code{glm()} twin with the fixed effects as dummies (a \code{feols()}
fit already matches \code{lm()}), and
\code{summary(fit, ssc = fixest::ssc(K.adj = FALSE))} gives the \code{glm()}
values.

\strong{\code{quantreg::rq}.} Its own estimator family, not the sandwich
vocabulary:
\itemize{
\item \code{"classical"} resolves to the heteroskedasticity-robust \code{"nid"}
sandwich (Hendricks-Koenker, Hall-Sheather bandwidth), quantreg's
own large-sample default and the analog of Stata's \code{vce(robust)}.
\item \code{"iid"}: the Koenker-Bassett homoskedastic estimator, as
Stata's \code{vce(iid)}.
\item \code{"ker"}: the Powell kernel sandwich.
\item \code{"rank"}: rank-score inversion, which gives intervals only: the
SE, t and p cells render as dashes, and the AME columns, which need
a variance matrix, are refused.
\item \code{"bootstrap"}: quantreg's native \code{boot.rq} (\code{bsmethod = "xy"}).
With \verb{cluster =}, the wild gradient cluster bootstrap of Hagemann
(2017), the route available for clustered data with \code{rq}. With very
few clusters, its standard errors read against normal critical
values reject too often (Hagemann 2017). A weighted \code{rq} fit
refuses it: the resamplers do not carry the weights.
}
\code{cluster} with any other \code{rq} estimator is refused.

\strong{\code{AER::tobit}.} A \code{tobit} fit is a \code{survreg} model, and takes the
same estimator: \code{"CR1"} from \code{\link[sandwich:vcovCL]{sandwich::vcovCL()}}, the CR0 sandwich
scaled by \eqn{G/(G-1)}{G/(G-1)}, \code{G} the number of clusters. The other
\verb{CR*} tokens and \verb{HC*} are refused. Under \code{"CR1"}, a nested hierarchy
is tested by the Wald chi-square of the added coefficients on the
robust matrix. The RMST and risk-difference columns of \code{survreg} are
refused for it: a tobit models a censored response, not a time to
event. Its AME is computed on the latent (uncensored) outcome, whose
mean is the linear predictor, and the note says so.

\strong{Hierarchical comparison} (\code{nested = TRUE}). A quantile hierarchy
gets \code{c("f_change", "p_change")}, the Wald-type test
\code{quantreg::anova.rq()} reports, under \code{"nid"} and \code{"rank"}; under
\code{"iid"} and \code{"ker"} it is a Wald F of the added coefficients, and under
\code{"bootstrap"} a Wald chi-square. A \code{fixest} hierarchy is compared by
likelihood-ratio test; a fit estimated with a non-iid vcov gets a Wald
F when its coefficient tests are t (\code{feols}, and \code{feglm} with a family
whose dispersion is estimated) and a Wald chi-square when they are z
(\code{fepois}, \code{femlm}, \code{fenegbin}, and \code{feglm} with the binomial or Poisson
family). See \emph{Hierarchical (nested) model comparison} in
\code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

\code{lm_robust} defaults to
\code{c("nobs", "r2", "adj_r2")}, \code{iv_robust} to
\code{"nobs"} alone, and an IV \code{fixest::feols()} shows neither R-squared nor
AIC: the 2SLS R-squared is not the classical one. Asked for, it is the
R-squared of \code{ivreg()}, on the structural residuals. The AIC and BIC of
a \code{fixest} model are those of its \code{lm()}, \code{glm()} or \code{MASS::glm.nb()}
twin with the fixed effects as dummies; see
\link{table_regression_tokens}.
\itemize{
\item \code{"fixed_effects"}: the \verb{Fixed effects:} block, on by default.
\item \code{"within_r2"}: the R-squared of the model with the fixed effects
partialed out, a default for \code{feols} except an IV one, on request
for \code{estimatr}. GLM-family \code{fixest} fits report fixest's McFadden
\code{pr2} instead.
\item \code{"n_groups"}: one \verb{N (<factor>)} row per absorbed factor, on
request.
}
Named in the call, \code{"fixed_effects"}, \code{"within_r2"} and \code{"n_groups"}
are refused when no model of the table absorbs fixed effects, a
\code{fixest} fit without \verb{| fe} included. \code{"within_r2"} is refused too when
no model of the table has a value for it (an IV \code{feols()}, a GLM-family
\code{fixest} fit).
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

\itemize{
\item Every \code{vcov} other than \code{"classical"} for \code{estimatr} and
\code{fixest} fits: the variance is chosen at fit time (see above).
\item For \code{rq}: \verb{HC*}, since quantile regression has no working
residuals or hat values (\code{"nid"} is the heteroskedasticity-robust
estimator here, a sparsity-based quantile sandwich, not an HC
variant); \verb{CR*}, since there is no cluster-robust sandwich for it
(use \code{"bootstrap"} with \verb{cluster =}); and \code{"jackknife"}, whose
leave-one-out form is inconsistent for quantiles: the quantile
objective is not differentiable, so dropping one observation can
move the fit discontinuously.
\item \code{nested = TRUE} across two \code{fixest} fits that absorbed
different fixed effects. fixest counts every absorbed level as an
estimated parameter, so a test across two \verb{| fe} structures
measures the change in the absorbed effects together with the
change in the coefficients, and the two cannot be separated. Hold
the \verb{| fe} part fixed across the hierarchy.
\item The AME of a \code{fixest} GLM with fixed effects, and of a \code{feols()}
with fixed effects fitted with \code{lean = TRUE} (see \emph{Engines}).
\item A \code{fixest} fit with fixed effects only: it has no coefficient
row to show.
}
}

\examples{
if (requireNamespace("quantreg", quietly = TRUE)) {
  fit <- quantreg::rq(wellbeing_score ~ age + sex, tau = 0.5,
                      data = sochealth)
  print(table_regression(fit))

  # The homoskedastic estimator instead of the default "nid".
  print(table_regression(fit, vcov = "iid"))
}

}
